---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import photos from '../data/photos.json';
import papers from '../data/publications.json';
const get=(id)=>photos.find(p=>p.id===id);
const img=(id,width=1400)=>get(id).src+`?format=${width}w`;
const featured=papers.filter(p=>p.featured);
const methods=['Hierarchical statistical modeling','Causal inference and econometrics','Large-scale social–ecological data integration','Direct behavioral observation','Qualitative field research','Machine learning and computational tools'];
---
<BaseLayout title="Science | Bing Lin — Conservation, behavior & policy" description="Bing Lin's research program, selected publications, grants, teaching and mentoring in conservation science, behavioral science and environmental policy." theme="light" image={img('turtle',1500)}>
  <section class="science-opener">
    <img src={img('diver-blue',2200)} alt={get('diver-blue').alt} fetchpriority="high"/>
    <div class="science-opener-shade"></div>
    <div class="science-opener-content"><span class="overline">BING LIN / SCIENCE</span><h1>Nature &<br /><em>Human Nature.</em></h1><p>Bridging ecology, behavioral science, and policy to understand and reconcile tensions between Nature and Human Nature—for people and oceans.</p></div>
    <a class="science-scroll" href="#research-vision">EXAMINE THE QUESTIONS ↓</a>
  </section>
  <div class="science-page-nav" aria-label="Science page sections"><div class="page-nav-inner"><span>RESEARCH NOTES / 2026</span><nav><a href="#research-vision">Vision</a><a href="#research-program">Research</a><a href="#publications">Publications</a><a href="#leadership">Leadership</a><a href="#teaching">Teaching</a><a href="#resources">CV & links</a></nav></div></div>

  <section class="science-introduction paper-section" id="research-vision"><div class="science-left-label"><span class="micro-label">01 / THE BIG QUESTIONS</span><div class="science-side-marker">FROM THE REEF TO THE RULES THAT GOVERN IT</div></div><div class="science-body" data-reveal><h2>What makes some places <em>thrive?</em></h2><p class="research-opening">Why do some ecosystems and communities fare better than we would expect, even under extraordinary pressures? What makes conservation succeed? And what can we learn from the exceptions?</p><p>I’m a postdoctoral researcher at the University of Sydney’s Thriving Oceans Research Hub, working with Professor Josh Cinner to identify coral reef “bright spots” and investigate the social–ecological conditions that sustain them. Through large-scale comparative analysis and fieldwork, I ask how environmental conditions, governance, and human decisions combine to shape different outcomes for people and nature.</p><p>Before moving to Sydney, I completed my Ph.D. at Princeton University with Professor David Wilcove. My research examined the human dimensions of biodiversity conservation—from nature-based tourism and the impacts of recreational diving to marine aquarium trade and environmental policy.</p><p>Across these projects, a question keeps returning: <strong>How can we make the right choice the easy choice for stakeholders?</strong></p><div class="science-quicklinks"><a href="/docs/Bing_Lin_Academic_CV_2026.pdf" download>ACADEMIC CV ↗</a><a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=en&user=WHSrGxIAAAAJ" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GOOGLE SCHOLAR ↗</a><a href="#publications">SELECTED PAPERS ↓</a></div></div></section>

  <section class="science-process dark-section" aria-label="Research vision"><div class="process-head" data-reveal><p class="overline">A RESEARCH PROGRAM / FROM POSITIVE DEVIANCE TO PRACTICE</p><h2>Learning from<br /><em>what goes right.</em></h2><p>Our 2026 review in <em>Nature Ecology & Evolution</em> brings together more than 70 studies, distinguishes six approaches to identifying bright spots, and asks how exceptional outcomes can inform conservation elsewhere.</p></div><div class="process-steps"><article data-reveal><span class="step-no">I.</span><p>FIND</p><h3>Look for the exceptions.</h3><span>Identify systems whose ecological or social outcomes exceed expectations given the pressures they face.</span></article><article data-reveal><span class="step-no">II.</span><p>EXPLAIN</p><h3>Understand what makes them different.</h3><span>Investigate the institutions, behaviors, ecological processes, and histories associated with their success.</span></article><article data-reveal><span class="step-no">III.</span><p>LEARN</p><h3>Ask what can travel.</h3><span>Consider whether—and under which conditions—insights from exceptional places can help elsewhere.</span></article></div><p class="process-credit">IN CONVERSATION WITH / <em>Unifying bright spots research for people and nature</em> · <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-026-03180-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nature Ecology & Evolution, 2026 ↗</a></p></section>

  <section class="science-agenda paper-section" id="research-program"><div class="science-left-label"><span class="micro-label">02 / THE PROGRAM</span></div><div class="science-body"><h2>Three connected <em>lines of inquiry.</em></h2><div class="research-line" data-reveal><figure><img src={img('reef-wall',1400)} alt={get('reef-wall').alt} loading="lazy"/><figcaption>01 / PEOPLE AND REEFS</figcaption></figure><div><span class="subtle-n">01</span><h3>Bright spots & exceptional outcomes</h3><p>Why do some social–ecological systems outperform expectations? I’m interested in what positive deviance can reveal about resilience, sustainability, and the conditions under which success can be sustained or adapted.</p><a class="text-arrow" href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-026-03180-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RELATED RESEARCH ↗</a></div></div><div class="research-line" data-reveal><figure><img src={img('diver-blue',1400)} alt={get('diver-blue').alt} loading="lazy"/><figcaption>02 / BEHAVIOR IN THE FIELD</figcaption></figure><div><span class="subtle-n">02</span><h3>Human behavior & conservation</h3><p>Why do good intentions so often fail to produce good environmental outcomes? From recreational diving to the choices people make around biodiversity, my work applies behavioral science to understand what people actually do—and why.</p><a class="text-arrow" href="https://doi.org/10.1111/con4.70055" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RELATED RESEARCH ↗</a></div></div><div class="research-line" data-reveal><figure><img src={img('anemonefish',1400)} alt={get('anemonefish').alt} loading="lazy"/><figcaption>03 / CONSERVATION & GOVERNANCE</figcaption></figure><div><span class="subtle-n">03</span><h3>Evidence for better conservation policy</h3><p>How can better ecological evidence, risk assessments, institutions, and incentives make conservation more effective and equitable? My work on reef tourism, wildlife markets, and the aquarium trade approaches that question from different angles.</p><a class="text-arrow" href="https://doi.org/10.1111/cobi.70381" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">RELATED RESEARCH ↗</a></div></div></div></section>

  <section class="science-papers" id="publications"><div class="paper-wrap"><div class="science-section-head"><div><p class="overline dark">03 / SELECTED SCHOLARSHIP</p><h2>The published <em>work.</em></h2></div><p>Peer-reviewed research across ecology, conservation, behavior, and environmental policy. The full record lives on <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=en&user=WHSrGxIAAAAJ" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google Scholar ↗</a>.</p></div><div class="featured-papers">{featured.map((p,i)=><article class:list={['feature-paper',i===0&&'feature-paper-lead']} data-reveal><img src={img(p.image,1800)} alt={get(p.image).alt} loading="lazy"/><div class="feature-paper-text"><span>{p.journal} / {p.year}</span><h3>{p.title}</h3><p>{p.authors}</p>{p.summary && <p class="feature-summary">{p.summary}</p>}<a href={p.doi} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">READ THE PAPER <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></article>)}</div><h3 class="all-publications-heading">Complete selected publications <span>({papers.length})</span></h3><div class="paper-list">{papers.map((p,i)=><article class="paper-record"><span class="pub-year">{p.year}</span><div><a href={p.doi} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{p.title}</a><p>{p.authors} · <em>{p.journal}</em></p></div><span class="pub-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></article>)}</div></div></section>

  <section class="science-journal-cover"><div class="journal-cover-grid"><div data-reveal><span class="overline">RESEARCH IN VIEW</span><h2>Making<br />the <em>cover.</em></h2><p>My work with colleagues on coral reefs and coastal tourism in Hawaii appeared on the cover of <em>Nature Sustainability</em>. It asks how the places people visit, the economies they support, and the ecosystems underneath them are bound together.</p><a class="underlined-link" href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41893-022-01021-4" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">READ THE PAPER ↗</a></div><figure data-reveal><img src="https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/6584fe2f3bbafd4de705d74f/917f6659-bb52-4a91-bfa0-84fef22c93a6/cover.tif.jpg?format=750w" alt="Nature Sustainability journal cover featuring reef-tourism research" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>NATURE SUSTAINABILITY / COVER FEATURE</figcaption></figure></div></section>

  <section class="science-leadership paper-section" id="leadership"><div class="science-left-label"><span class="micro-label">04 / THE WORK BEHIND THE WORK</span></div><div class="science-body"><h2>In the field.<br /><em>Across disciplines.</em></h2><p class="section-intro">I've worked on conservation questions at multiple scales—from longitudinal field research in Ethiopia to international reef studies, direct observations of diver behavior, and large global datasets. Different tools are useful for different kinds of questions.</p><div class="methods-block"><h3>Approaches & methods</h3><ul>{methods.map(m=><li>{m}</li>)}</ul></div><div class="evidence-grid"><article><span class="micro-label">RESEARCH LEADERSHIP</span><h3>Current and previous roles</h3><p>Founding fellow of the University of Sydney’s Thriving Oceans Research Hub; postdoctoral research at Princeton; former Field Manager of the Guassa Gelada Research Project in Ethiopia and Marine Site Manager for Operation Wallacea in Dominica.</p></article><article><span class="micro-label">COMPETITIVE SUPPORT</span><h3>Research grants & fellowships</h3><p>Support includes the Ferenbach Research Fund (2022–24), Princeton Institute for International and Regional Studies research awards, the Princeton Dean’s Completion Award, and the Mary and Randall Hack ’69 Graduate Award for Water and the Environment.</p><p>The Alfred P. Sloan Foundation also supports my forthcoming book through a 2026–28 book grant. This is a book award, distinct from research project funding.</p></article><article><span class="micro-label">RESEARCH COLLABORATION</span><h3>Working across boundaries</h3><p>Ecologists, behavioral scientists, policymakers, community partners, and field researchers bring different kinds of knowledge. My work moves between quantitative analysis and the social contexts that make conservation outcomes possible.</p></article><article><span class="micro-label">PUBLIC ENGAGEMENT</span><h3>Beyond the journal</h3><p>My research has reached audiences through science communication and environmental reporting, and my work in photography offers another way of making complicated environmental problems visible.</p><p>I received a 2025 National Academies Eric and Wendy Schmidt Award for Excellence in Science Communications.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="science-teaching" id="teaching"><div class="teaching-grid"><div data-reveal><p class="overline dark">05 / TEACHING & MENTORING</p><h2>Passing the<br /><em>curiosity on.</em></h2><p>I've taught across classrooms and field stations, and I currently supervise graduate research. The most rewarding part is helping people develop the skills—and the confidence—to ask their own questions.</p><a class="text-arrow" href="/docs/Bing_Lin_Academic_CV_2026.pdf" download>SEE MY ACADEMIC CV ↗</a></div><div class="teaching-records" data-reveal><div><span>SUPERVISION</span><h3>Research students</h3><p>Doctoral supervision, University of Sydney (Lily Wright, ongoing); master's supervision, University of Sydney (Peter Hlusek, class of 2026); undergraduate thesis mentoring, Princeton (Bryan To, class of 2022).</p></div><div><span>TEACHING</span><h3>Princeton & the field</h3><p>Assistantship in Instruction for <em>The Environment: Science and Policy</em> (2023) and <em>Human Factors 2.0</em> (2021); Head Instructor, Marine Ecology and Conservation, Operation Wallacea (2018).</p></div><div><span>ACADEMIC SERVICE</span><h3>Supporting the discipline</h3><p>Peer review for <em>Science</em>, <em>PNAS</em>, <em>The Lancet Planetary Health</em>, and other journals; Princeton environmental policy and student-service roles.</p></div></div></div></section>

  <section class="science-resources dark-section" id="resources"><div class="resources-head"><span class="overline">06 / FOR COLLEAGUES, STUDENTS & SEARCH COMMITTEES</span><h2>Go a little <em>deeper.</em></h2><p>The papers, the background, and a way to get in touch.</p></div><div class="resource-row"><a href="/docs/Bing_Lin_Academic_CV_2026.pdf" download><small>01 / PDF</small><strong>Academic CV</strong><span>DOWNLOAD ↗</span></a><a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=en&user=WHSrGxIAAAAJ" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><small>02 / SCHOLARSHIP</small><strong>Google Scholar</strong><span>VIEW ↗</span></a><a href="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5905-9512" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><small>03 / RESEARCH ID</small><strong>ORCID</strong><span>VIEW ↗</span></a><a href="/contact/"><small>04 / COLLABORATION</small><strong>Get in touch</strong><span>WRITE ↗</span></a></div><p class="resources-disclosure">For a fuller publication list, grant history, and academic appointments, see the downloadable CV. Pending proposals are not listed as awarded grants.</p></section>
</BaseLayout>
